.TP
.B pgrep ssh
Print PIDs of processes whose \fIcomm\fR contains "ssh" (so this
matches \fBssh\fR, \fBsshd\fR, \fBssh\-agent\fR, etc.).
.TP
.B pgrep \-x ssh
Match exactly "ssh" — \fBsshd\fR and \fBssh\-agent\fR are excluded.
.TP
.B pgrep \-f 'python3 .*manage\\.py'
Match the full command line. Useful when you need to distinguish
several processes with the same short name.
.TP
.B pgrep \-U 1000
Match every process owned by real UID 1000. With no pattern, the
filter alone selects the result set.
.TP
.B pgrep \-l \-U 1000
Same as above but also print the process name on each line.
.TP
.B pgrep \-c \-U 1000
Print just the count of matching processes.
.TP
.B pgrep \-P 1
Match every process whose parent is PID 1 (typically systemd or
init).
.TP
.B pgrep \-d , \-x bash
Match all \fBbash\fR processes and print their PIDs comma-separated
on one line, suitable for splicing into another command.
.TP
.B kill $(pgrep \-x my\-daemon)
The traditional pgrep-pipe-kill idiom. Prefer \fBpkill\fR(1) for the
same effect with one command and atomic-ish semantics.
.TP
.B pgrep \-r D
Match processes currently in uninterruptible sleep (state \fBD\fR) —
typically blocked on disk I/O.
